1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
You remember what.

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,200
Keep the why.

3
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:08,000
For the record, this is why I did it.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,947
Create a project and give the next session a clear intention.

5
00:00:14,947 --> 00:00:20,000
Your work journal needs no account or wallet.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,333
Choose a focus duration and start.

7
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:32,444
This demonstration finishes early after a few seconds.

8
00:00:32,444 --> 00:00:36,000
Record what actually changed.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,923
Save the result and a specific next action.

10
00:00:40,923 --> 00:00:44,000
Saving is an explicit choice.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,571
Open the project book, then return to the bookmark.

12
00:00:49,571 --> 00:00:57,000
The next session starts with your previous next action already filled in.

13
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
The saved next action is now the next intention.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
This is the same text from the previous record.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A transaction keeps the event.

16
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,000
Your journal keeps the reason.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,750
The journal requests read-only wallet access.

18
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:21,000
This emulator has no compatible wallet installed, so it shows a clear message and keeps the local record.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,667
The next sequence uses a labeled sample transaction.

20
00:01:23,667 --> 00:01:28,000
It demonstrates the actual app interface and local storage, not a live trade.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,333
Write the reason, then the plan.

22
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:35,222
Each prompt is optional.

23
00:01:35,222 --> 00:01:41,000
These are demonstration notes attached to sample activity.

24
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Choose an emotion and leave a next action.

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The journal does not score returns or reward trading volume.

26
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Save the reflection on this device, then reopen the same record from the sample activity.

27
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,727
The saved reason, plan and next action remain.

28
00:02:02,727 --> 00:02:07,455
Open Reflect to add what you learned later.

29
00:02:07,455 --> 00:02:11,000
The fixture stays visibly labeled throughout.

30
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,375
The later reflection is saved in the same local record.

31
00:02:15,375 --> 00:02:18,000
This demonstrates persistence with sample activity.

32
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,438
Notes stay on this device.

33
00:02:21,438 --> 00:02:29,000
Settings offer a portable JSON export, optional reminders and reduced motion.

34
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,600
English is the default, with seven more language choices.

35
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,000
Your own writing is never translated.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
In a separate manual emulator test, the official Solana mock wallet presented a native connection approval.

37
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,083
Approval returned a public account and a mainnet activity read returned an empty history.

38
00:02:45,083 --> 00:02:48,000
This verifies connection and reading, not a live swap reflection.

39
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Physical Seeker, Seed Vault and a supported live event-to-reflection run remain to be verified.

40
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,842
Leave tomorrow a starting point.

41
00:02:54,842 --> 00:02:58,158
For the record, this is why I did it.

42
00:02:58,158 --> 00:03:00,000
Try the preview at workroom-seeker-6984.web.app.
